From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] PackageManager: the handler watchdog scales with ro.hw_timeout_multiplier

An application is installed on PackageManagerService's handler
(processPendingInstall), and installPackageLI compiles it there with the
install filter (pm.dexopt.install, speed). The watchdog declares that
handler wedged after WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT, ten minutes, and then kills
system_server; the dexopt wake lock is held for one minute more.

Ten minutes is generous on phone hardware. On the single Cortex-A9 of the
PW3, dex2oat compiles WeRead (13 dex files, 70.5 MB) in 1199 s, 20 minutes
(Test V40.1, `cmd package compile -m speed -f`, which runs on a binder
thread), so installing it the way its publisher describes (copy the APK
over USB, open it in Files) would restart the framework half way. That
compile also outlived the wake lock: "WakeLock *dexopt* time out.
Operation took 1198928 ms." The deadline now scales like the application
deadlines of ActivityManagerService (0012): the product's multiplier is 4,
which gives 40 minutes, and the wake lock 41.
---
--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/pm/PackageManagerService.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/pm/PackageManagerService.java
@@ -485,8 +485,11 @@
      * our handler thread is wedged.  The usual default for such things is one
      * minute but we sometimes do very lengthy I/O operations on this thread,
      * such as installing multi-gigabyte applications, so ours needs to be longer.
+     * PW3: installing also compiles the application here, which takes many
+     * minutes on slow hardware; scaled like ActivityManagerService's deadlines.
      */
-    static final long WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT = 1000*60*10;     // ten minutes
+    static final long WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT = 1000*60*10     // ten minutes
+            * Math.max(1, SystemProperties.getInt("ro.hw_timeout_multiplier", 1));
 
     /**
      * Wall-clock timeout (in milliseconds) after which we *require* that an fstrim
